{% if node %}
### Mandatory Node package scripts

At `[[ node_root ]]`: lint, typecheck, test:unit, test:integration,
test:coverage and build. Test actual HTTP/contract/service behavior,
including negative and recovery paths.
{% endif %}
{% if python %}
### Python quality contracts

Locked uv project includes Ruff, mypy, pytest/pytest-cov,
`pip-licenses` and the application dependencies. The PEP 517 build
backend must be pinned in `[build-system]` **and** installed as a
locked dev dependency: `uv build --no-build-isolation` deliberately
fails when that backend is missing instead of resolving a new version.
Static checks, unit/integration tests, coverage and build must pass.
{% endif %}
{% if shell %}
### Shell quality contracts

ShellCheck and shfmt audit `.sh` and extensionless Bash/sh shebang
scripts from one inventory. Bats tests real executable behavior.
Missing source and tests are errors, not an implicit skip.
{% endif %}

## Owner and agent authority

GitHub parent/sub-issues, actual Git/PR and current build logs own
execution facts. No second task database, made-up agent runtime or
transcript-copying handoff. Prototype, design and final owner
interaction checks are separate from technical CI; only native harness
approval controls can authorize consequential actions.

## Standard updates and rollback

Use Copier's own version-aware update functionality against a reviewed
Rill source ref. Copier merges template diffs and product evolution;
unresolved conflicts stay visible. Keep the product in an isolated,
clean Git branch or worktree and test before merging. Standard rollback
is native Git revert/restore of this scoped change.

The Rill template contains **no** arbitrary template tasks or migrations
and must not need the Copier unsafe switch. A copied workflow without
executed checks and a real product review is **not** verified adoption.

## Supply-chain evidence

The generated security job installs actual locked dependencies, scans
vulnerabilities/secrets/misconfigurations, blocks restricted or forbidden
licenses and records a CycloneDX SBOM plus dependency-license reports
as short-lived GitHub Actions artifacts. Trivy cannot determine uv.lock
package licenses by reading that file alone: the Python license inventory
uses the locked `pip-licenses` tool on installed project dependencies.
Unknown licenses require explicit human review, never a silent PASS.
Source and lockfile scans occur before installation; generated license
and SBOM reports are uploaded before a blocking classification check.
Compound SPDX expressions and unsupported classifications need
recorded human review; the exact-match GPL/AGPL block cannot certify
all license expressions automatically.
